// Sealed Ward (Lament) - the same ward, with the bass leading the harmony
// instead of pedalling under it. A second track beside sealed-ward, not a
// replacement: that one is an ostinato piece, this one is a progression.
//
// Riff and verse:  Dm | C | Bb | A7 | Gm | Bb C | Dm Bb | A
// A lament descent in the bass (D C Bb A), one chord a bar, then the harmonic
// rhythm doubles into the cadence and the last bar walks up chromatically into
// D. The bass is written as a player would: the root on the downbeat, the other
// chord tones and passing notes between, rests, and ghosts marked `+` - on this
// ensemble's Swag Bass those are the dead thud of the string, not a short note.
//
// Rite:  Bb | C | Am | Dm | Gm | Eb | Bb C | A
// The Eb is the flat second, the darkest chord in the key, set up by Gm and
// falling onto the dominant. The bass pumps octaves here.
//
// The bridge (`break`) is sealed-ward itself: its pedal cell, unmoving, under
// chords that only darken, the hook's head an octave down on the organ, and a
// second half that adds weight rather than light. It stays in D minor - a
// semitone lift and celesta answers were tried there and changed the tone of
// the piece.

title: Sealed Ward (Lament)
ensemble: tonewheel
parts: lead guitar keys bass pad perc
tempo: 132
meter: 4/4
key: d minor
order: intro [riff verse rite break reprise] out

